## st-story | Stories from statistics | stats | 0 | - | st-samp
story 4 | Florence Nightingale was a statistician
- In the Crimean War, she showed more soldiers died from disease in hospitals than from battle wounds.
- Her polar area ("rose") diagrams persuaded officials to improve sanitation.
- *She became the first female member of the Royal Statistical Society, in 1859.*
> Student's t-test came from a brewery

story 4 | The t-test was invented at Guinness
- William Gosset worked at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, testing barley with small samples.
- Guinness didn't let employees publish under their names, so he used the pen name "Student" (1908).
- *Student's t-test is now used in labs everywhere.*
> Fisher and the lady tasting tea

story 4 | The lady tasting tea
- A colleague of Ronald Fisher claimed she could tell if milk was poured before or after the tea.
- Fisher designed a randomised test with eight cups. She reportedly got them all right.
- *It became the opening example of his 1935 book on experimental design.*
> Next: more economics stories

## ec-story | Economics stories | econ | 0 | - | ec-hist
story 4 | The Phillips machine computed the economy with water
- 1949: economist Bill Phillips built a machine of tanks and pipes where flowing water represented money in the economy.
- Valves modelled taxes, savings and spending.
- *A working one is on display at the Science Museum in London.*
> The Lipstick index

story 3 | Do people buy more lipstick in recessions?
- Estée Lauder's chairman suggested lipstick sales rise in downturns as an affordable luxury.
- The evidence is mixed, and the index is more anecdote than law.
- *Economic folk indicators are fun but often unreliable.*
> The Big Mac index

explain 3 | The Big Mac index
- The Economist compares burger prices across countries to judge whether currencies look over- or undervalued.
- It's based on purchasing power parity: the same thing should cost the same everywhere.
- *Started in 1986 as a light-hearted guide.*
